---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const EP = "server/src/iki_server/runs/egress_proxy.py";
const E = "server/src/iki_server/runs/egress.py";
const O = "server/src/iki_server/runs/outside.py";
const S = "server/src/iki_server/runs/secrets.py";
const Q = "server/src/iki_server/runs/queue.py";

const H = {
  short: "What a run can reach, and with what",
  how: "Through the egress proxy",
  rules: "Hosts, outside APIs, secrets and the network policy",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Workers and run pods reach only DNS, the server, object storage and the egress proxy. A run's
      command reaches the internet only through the proxy, only the hosts its action declares, only
      over HTTPS, and only while its attempt holds its lease. An outside API is approved per
      project; the proxy calls it with the organization's key, which never reaches the run. Secrets
      are declared by name and read from the deployment's run secrets at each lease (<Adr
        n={[53, 54, 130, 155]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      For an action that declares hosts or calls outside APIs, the lease's payload carries a proxy
      URL whose user is the job's ID and whose password is derived from its lease token with HMAC,
      so it stops working when the lease ends. The worker gives it to the command as{" "}
      <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> and hides it, with the lease token and the secrets, in everything the
      command prints (<Path p={Q} sym="def _proxy(" />, <Path p={E} sym="def _password(" />, <Path
        p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/job.rs"
        sym="pub(crate) fn hidden("
      />). Vm-tier machines and Slurm compute nodes reach the proxy at addresses of their own.
    </p>
    <p>For each <code>CONNECT host:443</code>, the proxy:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>checks the credentials belong to an attempt that holds its lease, and that its action declares the host (<Path p={E} sym="def decide(" />);</li>
      <li>resolves the host itself and connects only to public addresses, or networks the operator allowed, at the address it checked, so a rebinding answer can't reach inside;</li>
      <li>reads the TLS ClientHello and closes the tunnel unless it names that host;</li>
      <li>closes the tunnel when the attempt ends, or after five idle minutes;</li>
      <li>keeps, with the attempt, each host reached or refused, with connections and bytes, for the run's page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Plain HTTP and other ports are refused. The proxy is off by default in the chart; until it's
      on, actions that declare hosts are unavailable, and the launch says why (<Path p={EP} />, <Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py"
        sym="def availability("
      />, <Path p="deploy/helm/iki/templates/egress-proxy-deployment.yaml" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Declared hosts" open>
        <p>
          An action lists hosts under <code>network</code>: an exact name, or <code>*.example.com</code>{" "}
          for its subdomains at any depth but not the domain itself (<Path p={E} sym="def declares(" />).
          An entry can be <code>{"{settings.<name>}"}</code>, a hostname setting the deployment's
          build fills in, so one plugin can reach each lab's own system; an unset one reaches nothing
          (<Adr n="0083" />). An action that can't run without a setting is unavailable until it's set
          (<Adr n="0169" />). An action that declares hosts or outside APIs isn't retried by default,
          since a retry could repeat what it did elsewhere, such as placing an order (<Path
            p="spec/src/iki_spec/actions.py"
            sym="retry_max="
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Outside APIs are approved per project">
        <p>
          A plugin names each API it calls, with its host, purpose and credential, and each action
          says what it sends. An Org Admin approves each API per project, in the web app only; a
          launch without the approval is refused with 409 <code>outside_api_not_approved</code>, and
          the person launching consents to what's sent. For an API host the proxy doesn't tunnel: it
          ends TLS itself, checks the approval again on every call, adds the key from the run
          secrets, and records each call and its cost. A command that submitted a long job parks, and
          the run resumes in a new attempt once the job is done. APIs are refused on the self-hosted
          tier, whose machine owner could read the key (<Adr n="0130" />, <Path p={O} sym="def check_approved(" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/api_gateway.py" />). Setup builds call none (<Adr
            n="0141"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Secrets come at each lease">
        <p>
          An action declares the secrets it needs by name. Each is a file in the run secrets
          directory, named for the plugin's label and the secret (<code>iki__dna.NCBI_API_KEY</code>),
          so one plugin's secrets are never another's. They're read each time a job is leased, so a
          rotated secret reaches the next run without a restart, and given to the command as
          environment variables; the <code>input.json</code> it reads doesn't hold them. Only their
          names are kept or shown. A required one that isn't set makes the action unavailable (<Adr
            n="0054"
          />, <Path p={S} sym="def for_run(" />, <Path p={Q} sym="def payload(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The network policy">
        <p>
          The chart's NetworkPolicy lets workers and run pods reach DNS, the server, object storage,
          the egress proxy and what <code>networkPolicy.egress.workers</code> adds, nothing else. The
          operator lists where the server and object storage are (<Adr n="0155" />, <Path
            p="deploy/helm/iki/templates/networkpolicy.yaml"
          />). The rest of the chart is on <PageLink to="The Helm chart" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <h3>Routes for outside APIs</h3>
    <Routes tags={["Outside APIs"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={EP} /></li>
          <li><Path p={E} /></li>
          <li><Path p={O} /></li>
          <li><Path p={S} /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0053" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0054" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0083" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0130" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0155" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
